1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:05,349
If the largest stars we know took the Sun's place, you would be living inside one.

2
00:00:05,349 --> 00:00:09,494
Earth, Mars, even Jupiter, all orbiting deep inside a single star.

3
00:00:09,494 --> 00:00:11,252
And here's the strange part.

4
00:00:11,252 --> 00:00:14,706
Most of that star is thinner than the air in your room.

5
00:00:15,406 --> 00:00:21,690
Today we put our Sun next to the biggest stars ever measured, and we'll feel the difference in five ways.

6
00:00:21,690 --> 00:00:26,418
A lineup, a flight, a jar, a beam of light, and a solar system swallowed whole.

7
00:00:26,418 --> 00:00:28,512
Then the two questions nobody asks.

8
00:00:28,512 --> 00:00:31,325
How do you even measure a star you can't reach?

9
00:00:31,325 --> 00:00:33,539
And why can't a star grow any bigger?

10
00:00:34,240 --> 00:00:36,138
The Largest Star versus the Sun.

11
00:00:36,838 --> 00:00:38,901
First, give the Sun some respect.

12
00:00:38,901 --> 00:00:42,153
It's about one point four million kilometers across.

13
00:00:42,153 --> 00:00:45,716
About one point three million Earths would fit inside it.

14
00:00:45,716 --> 00:00:48,092
NASA calls it an average star in size.

15
00:00:48,092 --> 00:00:52,906
But most stars in our galaxy are red dwarfs, smaller and dimmer than the Sun.

16
00:00:52,906 --> 00:00:57,596
In a random crowd of stars, our Sun would stand out as one of the big ones.

17
00:00:58,296 --> 00:00:59,234
Now the problem.

18
00:00:59,234 --> 00:01:05,686
Every star except the Sun is so far away that, even in the biggest telescopes, it looks like a point of light.

19
00:01:05,686 --> 00:01:08,208
So how do you measure the width of a point?

20
00:01:08,208 --> 00:01:15,598
In December nineteen twenty, Albert Michelson and Francis Pease tried something clever on the giant telescope at Mount Wilson.

21
00:01:15,598 --> 00:01:22,167
They caught the light of Betelgeuse with two small mirrors set several meters apart, and combined the two beams.

22
00:01:22,868 --> 00:01:26,186
When the two beams line up, they make a pattern of stripes.

23
00:01:26,186 --> 00:01:31,136
Move the mirrors farther apart, and for a star with a real width, the stripes fade away.

24
00:01:31,136 --> 00:01:34,793
The distance where they vanish tells you how wide the star looks.

25
00:01:34,793 --> 00:01:38,168
For Betelgeuse, about zero point zero four seven arcseconds.

26
00:01:38,168 --> 00:01:42,443
That's like spotting the width of a coin from almost ninety kilometers away.

27
00:01:42,443 --> 00:01:46,718
It was the first time anyone measured the size of a star other than the Sun.

28
00:01:47,418 --> 00:01:50,437
The trick still works today, only much bigger.

29
00:01:50,437 --> 00:01:57,393
The European Southern Observatory's Very Large Telescope Interferometer combines four telescopes in Chile.

30
00:01:57,393 --> 00:02:02,906
In twenty twenty four, it took the first close up image of a star in another galaxy.

31
00:02:03,606 --> 00:02:04,851
So, way number one.

32
00:02:04,851 --> 00:02:05,571
The lineup.

33
00:02:05,571 --> 00:02:06,619
Here is the Sun.

34
00:02:06,619 --> 00:02:13,562
Next to it, R one three six a one, the most massive star we know, around two hundred times the Sun's mass.

35
00:02:13,562 --> 00:02:15,789
You'd expect it to be the biggest.

36
00:02:15,789 --> 00:02:16,378
It isn't.

37
00:02:16,378 --> 00:02:19,457
It's only a few dozen times wider than the Sun.

38
00:02:20,157 --> 00:02:26,465
Now Betelgeuse, the red shoulder of Orion, a star you can see tonight with your own eyes.

39
00:02:26,465 --> 00:02:32,277
A 2020 study puts it at about seven hundred and fifty times the radius of the Sun.

40
00:02:32,277 --> 00:02:36,954
Then V Y Canis Majoris, measured at around fourteen hundred times.

41
00:02:36,954 --> 00:02:48,365
And finally W O H G sixty four, in the Large Magellanic Cloud, which the European Southern Observatory describes as about two thousand times the size of the Sun.

42
00:02:48,365 --> 00:02:51,980
At this scale, the Sun is a dot you can barely see.

43
00:02:52,680 --> 00:02:53,616
Way number two.

44
00:02:53,616 --> 00:02:55,613
Drop them into our solar system.

45
00:02:55,613 --> 00:03:03,475
Betelgeuse, placed where the Sun is, would swallow Mercury, Venus, Earth, and Mars, with its surface out in the asteroid belt.

46
00:03:04,175 --> 00:03:06,216
V Y Canis Majoris goes further.

47
00:03:06,216 --> 00:03:10,826
NASA says it would reach out between the orbits of Jupiter and Saturn.

48
00:03:10,826 --> 00:03:13,591
Jupiter would be orbiting inside the star.

49
00:03:13,591 --> 00:03:17,147
From here on, let's use one round giant for our tests.

50
00:03:17,147 --> 00:03:22,415
A star fifteen hundred times wider than the Sun, the size of the record holders.

51
00:03:23,115 --> 00:03:24,161
Way number three.

52
00:03:24,161 --> 00:03:31,850
Take a passenger jet, cruising at nine hundred kilometers an hour, and fly it once around the Sun's equator without stopping.

53
00:03:31,850 --> 00:03:35,971
By our calculation, that trip takes about two hundred and two days.

54
00:03:35,971 --> 00:03:39,478
Seven months in a plane, just to go around our star once.

55
00:03:40,177 --> 00:03:41,670
Now fly around the giant.

56
00:03:41,670 --> 00:03:42,924
Same jet, same speed.

57
00:03:42,924 --> 00:03:45,132
About eight hundred and thirty years.

58
00:03:45,132 --> 00:03:54,385
If a flight had taken off when the first universities were opening in medieval Europe, it would still be in the air today, and it wouldn't have landed yet.

59
00:03:55,085 --> 00:03:56,525
But here's the paradox.

60
00:03:56,525 --> 00:03:59,653
A star that big must be unimaginably heavy, right?

61
00:03:59,653 --> 00:04:00,342
Not really.

62
00:04:00,342 --> 00:04:06,599
Our giant has maybe twenty times the mass of the Sun, spread over a volume billions of times larger.

63
00:04:06,599 --> 00:04:13,795
Do the math, and its average density is about a hundred and fifty thousand times thinner than the air at sea level.

64
00:04:14,495 --> 00:04:19,566
Astronomers sometimes call the outer layers of a red supergiant a red hot vacuum.

65
00:04:19,566 --> 00:04:28,394
Almost all of its mass is packed into a small, dense core, and around it floats a vast, glowing envelope of thin gas, puffing out like steam.

66
00:04:28,394 --> 00:04:30,773
That's why these stars are so fragile.

67
00:04:30,773 --> 00:04:35,593
Their outer layers are barely held by gravity, and they leak away into space.

68
00:04:36,293 --> 00:04:37,349
Way number four.

69
00:04:37,349 --> 00:04:37,877
The jar.

70
00:04:37,877 --> 00:04:40,583
How many Suns would fit inside our giant?

71
00:04:40,583 --> 00:04:43,223
Volume grows with the cube of the width.

72
00:04:43,223 --> 00:04:47,315
Fifteen hundred, times fifteen hundred, times fifteen hundred.

73
00:04:47,315 --> 00:04:49,691
About three point four billion Suns.

74
00:04:50,391 --> 00:04:56,562
And since one point three million Earths fit in each Sun, that's around four quadrillion Earths.

75
00:04:56,562 --> 00:04:58,684
A four followed by fifteen zeros.

76
00:04:58,684 --> 00:04:59,969
All inside one star.

77
00:05:00,669 --> 00:05:03,432
Way number five, and the biggest scale of all.

78
00:05:03,432 --> 00:05:03,792
Light.

79
00:05:03,792 --> 00:05:05,414
The fastest thing there is.

80
00:05:05,414 --> 00:05:11,059
A beam of light crosses the Sun, from one side to the other, in about four and a half seconds.

81
00:05:11,759 --> 00:05:15,182
Across our giant, that same beam needs almost two hours.

82
00:05:15,182 --> 00:05:22,030
Light could leave the Moon and reach Earth more than five thousand times in the time it takes to cross one star.

83
00:05:22,030 --> 00:05:24,964
Two hours, at the fastest speed in the universe.

84
00:05:25,664 --> 00:05:28,221
So which star is the largest, exactly?

85
00:05:28,221 --> 00:05:30,442
That answer just got complicated.

86
00:05:30,442 --> 00:05:35,556
In twenty twenty four, the image of W O H G sixty four showed something odd.

87
00:05:35,556 --> 00:05:42,688
The star was wrapped in an egg shaped cocoon of gas and dust, and it had been getting dimmer for a decade.

88
00:05:43,388 --> 00:05:59,585
Then, in twenty twenty six, a team led from the National Observatory of Athens reported in Nature Astronomy that W O H G sixty four had heated up by more than a thousand degrees, and seems to have turned from a red supergiant into a yellow hypergiant.

89
00:05:59,585 --> 00:06:02,296
It may have shed part of its outer layers.

90
00:06:02,296 --> 00:06:06,748
One of the biggest stars we ever measured is changing while we watch.

91
00:06:07,448 --> 00:06:09,328
That raises the real question.

92
00:06:09,328 --> 00:06:14,026
Why do the largest stars all stop around one or two thousand times the Sun?

93
00:06:14,026 --> 00:06:15,342
Why not ten thousand?

94
00:06:15,342 --> 00:06:21,794
In nineteen seventy nine, astronomers Roberta Humphreys and Kris Davidson noticed an invisible ceiling.

95
00:06:21,794 --> 00:06:26,368
Above a certain brightness, cool, bloated supergiants simply don't exist.

96
00:06:26,368 --> 00:06:29,187
It's now called the Humphreys Davidson limit.

97
00:06:29,887 --> 00:06:31,014
The likely reason?

98
00:06:31,014 --> 00:06:36,026
The heaviest stars shine so fiercely that their own light pushes their gas away.

99
00:06:36,026 --> 00:06:40,473
They blow off their outer layers before they can swell into red giants.

100
00:06:40,473 --> 00:06:46,675
A 2026 study from Armagh Observatory traced that ceiling to exactly this kind of runaway mass loss.

101
00:06:46,675 --> 00:06:54,506
So the most massive stars stay relatively compact, and the biggest stars are the medium heavyweights that puff up and let go.

102
00:06:55,205 --> 00:06:55,964
And our Sun?

103
00:06:55,964 --> 00:06:58,050
It will have its own giant phase.

104
00:06:58,050 --> 00:07:08,608
In about five billion years, NASA says, it will run out of hydrogen in its core and swell into a red giant, big enough to engulf Mercury and Venus, and possibly Earth.

105
00:07:08,608 --> 00:07:12,212
A small red giant, compared to the monsters on this list.

106
00:07:12,212 --> 00:07:14,235
Big enough to swallow our world.

107
00:07:14,935 --> 00:07:17,011
So, the largest star versus the Sun.

108
00:07:17,011 --> 00:07:18,452
Two thousand times wider.

109
00:07:18,452 --> 00:07:20,355
Billions of Suns of space inside.

110
00:07:20,355 --> 00:07:25,430
Eight centuries to fly around it, two hours for light to cross it, and thinner than air.

111
00:07:25,430 --> 00:07:28,083
A giant made mostly of glowing almost-nothing.

112
00:07:28,083 --> 00:07:31,081
But you don't need a giant star for extreme physics.

113
00:07:31,081 --> 00:07:37,367
Our own planet is spinning right now, at about sixteen hundred and seventy kilometers an hour at the equator.

114
00:07:37,367 --> 00:07:40,077
So what would happen if Earth suddenly stopped?

115
00:07:40,077 --> 00:07:40,769
That's next.
